<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 9/25</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use patterns in the limit computation of derivative to establish derivative rules.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Apply these rules to compute the derivatives of more complicated functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Derivative Rules</title>

            <p>
                Today starts Chapter 3 and our discussion of derivative rules. These are <q>shortcut</q> rules, all of which come from the definition of the derivative, to allow us to compute these derivatives more efficiently.
            </p>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                <nbsp />
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                These rules will not be allowed on your Test 1 next week. For those problems, you must use the limit definition, from the initial formula, to get the derivative. 
            </p>

            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                Where can we start? What kinds of functions should have easy derivatives?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Where can we start? What kinds of functions should have easy derivatives? Well, if a function is a straight line, the tangent line should just be the line itself. constant function, linear function
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            However, if the function is non-linear, we have more work to do.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            However, if the function is non-linear, we have more work to do. We've done a few powers of <m>x</m> before. We can try to find a pattern here.
        </p>


    </page>

    <page>
        <theorem xml:id="thm-power-rule">
            <title>The Power Rule</title>
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="1in">
                    For the function <m>f(x) = x^n</m> where <m>n</m> is any real number, we have <md>
                        f'(x) = 
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    For the function <m>f(x) = x^n</m> where <m>n</m> is any real number, we have <md>
                        f'(x) = nx^{n-1}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Compute the derivative of each of the following functions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = x^{35}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x)=x^{9/2}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = x^{\sqrt{2}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = x^{-1/3}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Exponential Rule</title>
            
            <p>
                Consider a function of the form <m>f(x) = b^x</m> for some base <m>b</m>. This doesn't work like the power rule, but the process of finding the derivative here has some nice simplification.
            </p>

            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                <nbsp />
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Set up the f(x+h) version. factor out the <m>b^x</m>. We define <m>e</m> to be the special number where the remaining limit is 1. Maybe connect to the other version of <m>e</m>.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <theorem xml:id="thm-exp-rule">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="1in">
                    If <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, then <md>
                        f'(x) = 
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    If <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, then <md>
                        f'(x) = e^x 
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Linearity of the Derivative</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                Another useful property of the derivative is that it is linear. This means that it plays nicely with addition and constant multiplication.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Another useful property of the derivative is that it is linear. This means that it plays nicely with addition and constant multiplication. This comes from the fact that the limit definition of the derivative does the same. In particular. f+g and cf all have nice derivatives.
            </p>
            
<example workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Compute the derivative of the function <md>
                f(x) = 7x^{20} - 6x^4 + \frac{3}{x^2} + 5e^x
            </md>.
            
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>


        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>

